VulkanNetwork — UDP, reliable datagrams and authenticated encryption.

Public headers:
  network/network.hpp
  network/endpoint.hpp         IPv4/IPv6 address and peer identity
  network/socket.hpp           low-level unencrypted UDP; internal transport use
  network/secure.hpp           automatic server identity, pinned DTLS 1.2
  network/stream.hpp           reliable messages, ordering-independent, congestion window
  network/session.hpp          reliable channels over a supplied datagram sender

Secure transport:
- Only supported game transport: authenticated and encrypted DTLS 1.2.
- net::secure::create(role, settings, port, bind_host) creates a secure endpoint.
- settings.storage_directory points at a writable per-user config directory.
  A server creates a persistent P-256 private key and a renewable certificate.
- Client settings.trust_identity is the configured host:port. On the first
  authenticated cryptographic handshake, the client's trust-on-first-use
  mechanism stores the server's SHA-256 public-key fingerprint.
- A later changed fingerprint is rejected. First-use trust cannot prove the
  intended server's identity against an active on-path attacker. An independent
  server identity channel is required for that additional guarantee.
- Client keys, client certificates, external OpenSSL scripts, CA distribution,
  plaintext and alternate network modes are not part of this implementation.
- All gameplay and reliable scene packets use the authenticated DTLS channel.
- net::session fragments, acknowledges and retries inside DTLS without owning
  its own UDP socket. Only net::secure binds to the UDP socket.
- Calling net::secure::connect after a session timeout creates a fresh DTLS
  handshake while retaining the trusted server key on the client's disk.
- The DTLS peer collection is single-thread-owned; callers must serialize use.

Protocol restrictions:
- Both endpoints must run the same version of the application and wire
  protocol. An earlier plaintext game client cannot join this server.
- DTLS does not authenticate individual clients; the game must enforce
  admission control, permissions and bans. A game-session token is not a
  user account identity.
- Bounded queues and DTLS cookies reduce trivial resource attacks but do not
  constitute DDoS protection. Nonblocking handshake and packet-loss testing
  are essential before deploying on an untrusted Internet network.
- DTLS 1.2 provides no automatic NAT traversal, dynamic DNS or relay.
- network/socket.hpp exposes raw UDP primitives needed internally by DTLS,
  but is not an alternate gameplay transport.

Build:
  The c build tool exports Git files without submodules. network/mbedtls_prepare.cmake
  is injected before the upstream Mbed TLS project configuration, fetches the
  Mbed TLS framework dependency at the exact upstream Gitlink revision
  dde0c4a0e448a0552f18817dcea633bb851fd288, and verifies its commit.
  This dependency needs Git access during first-time CMake configuration.
  In offline environments, pre-populate the pinned framework in the Mbed TLS
  source cache. Unavailable or mismatched framework sources fail the build.

  Mbed TLS 3.6.7 source is pinned and built as a static CMake dependency
  of VulkanNetwork (mbedtls, mbedx509, mbedcrypto).
  Mbed TLS is Apache-2.0 licensed.

